  "answer": "**Final status: passed.** Build, unit and component all pass in run `20261021T111948Z-bf62ff`. It took one fix, so only one of the two allowed fix rounds was used.\n\n**What failed:** On the first run, the build passed and the unit stage failed (exit code 2). Component tests were skipped because the run stopped there. About 40 unit tests failed on both net9.0 and net10.0. They were almost all the `SemanticMatcherTests.Apply_*` tests, plus five `ResultMatcherTests` that use semantic matchers. Each one threw `ArgumentException: Unknown semantic matcher '<name>'`.\n\n**Root cause:** In `src/ConfIT/Matching/SemanticMatcher.cs`, `ParseSpec` lowercased the matcher name, so `isUuid` became `isuuid`. The `BuiltIns` dictionary uses camelCase keys and looks them up case-sensitively. As a result, no built-in matcher was ever found and every semantic matcher threw. `ValidateSpecs` had the same mismatch.\n\n**What was changed** (only in `src/ConfIT/Matching/SemanticMatcher.cs`):\n1. The `BuiltIns` dictionary now ignores case when looking up names (`StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase`).\n2. `ParseSpec` no longer lowercases the matcher name.\n\n**Two side effects of the fix:**\n- **Custom matchers:** before, names were lowercased; now they are looked up with exactly the casing they were registered with. The custom-matcher tests pass.\n- **Clash check:** the check that stops a custom matcher reusing a built-in name now ignores case, so a name like `ISUUID` is also rejected.\n\nI didn't run anything or edit any files myself; subagents did all of it. Nothing was committed.",
